 "regions": [
  {
   "id": "r3",
   "type": "paragraph",
   "lines": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "text": "hari; not, as might be supposed from the name, the celebrated",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "text": "brother of Vikramâditya, but a grammarian and poet who was son",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 3,
     "text": "of S'rîdharasvâmî (sic; sed vide supra) as we are informed by",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 4,
     "text": "one of his scholiasts, Vidyâvinoda.”[*] In his Oxford catalogue,",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 5,
     "text": "",
     "end": "join"
    },
    {
     "n": 6,
     "text": "Prof. Aufrecht speaks of Bhartṛihari, “cujus liber grammaticus",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 7,
     "text": "minime vero Bhaṭṭikâvyam memoratur”.[†] Dr. Bhâu says that Bhaṭṭi",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 8,
     "text": "“is popularly believed to have been a son of Bhartṛihari.”[‡]",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 9,
     "text": "",
     "end": "join"
    },
    {
     "n": 10,
     "text": "Bohlen says: “They say that Vikrama in fact got possession of",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 11,
     "text": "the kingdom, and took to himself Bhaṭṭi as Prime Minister.",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 12,
     "text": "But in this again they seem to have gone wrong, con-",
     "end": "hyphen"
    },
    {
     "n": 13,
     "text": "founding both persons and times. For there exists a grammati-",
     "end": "hyphen"
    },
    {
     "n": 14,
     "text": "cal poem called Bhaṭṭikâvya, describing at the same time the ex-",
     "end": "hyphen"
    },
    {
     "n": 15,
     "text": "ploits of Râma, which has been attributed to a certain gram-",
     "end": "hyphen"
    },
    {
     "n": 16,
     "text": "marian belonging to a later age called Bhartṛihari, and from the",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 17,
     "text": "name of this poem, I think, Bhaṭṭi seems to have been considered",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 18,
     "text": "as the brother of this our Bhartṛihari.”[§] Lastly, Mr. Sashagiri",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 19,
     "text": "",
     "end": "join"
    },
    {
     "n": 20,
     "text": "S'âstrî says “King Vikramâditya is said to have been the son of",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 21,
     "text": "a Brâhmaṇ named Chandragupta who married four wives, one of",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 22,
     "text": "the Brâhmaṇ caste, another of the Kshatriya, the third of the",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 23,
     "text": "Vais'ya, the fourth of the S'ûdra caste. They were called Brâh-",
     "end": "hyphen"
    },
    {
     "n": 24,
     "text": "maṇî, Bhânumatî, Bhâgyavatî, and Sindhumatî. Each of the",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 25,
     "text": "",
     "end": "join"
    },
    {
     "n": 26,
     "text": "four bore him a son. Vararuchi was born of the first wife,",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 27,
     "text": "Vikramârka of the second, Bhaṭṭi of the third, and Bhartṛihari",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 28,
     "text": "of the fourth. Vikramârka became king, while Bhaṭṭi served",
     "end": "space"
    },
    {
     "n": 29,
     "text": "him in the capacity of Prime Minister.”[¶] From this conspectus of",
     "end": "space"
    }
   ],
   "uncertain": false,
   "note": null
  },
